SendEvent: leave the source devices' active inputs alone

write_send_event (the D-Bus method SendEvent) calls is_new_active_event,
which adds the event's capability to active_inputs on a press and removes
it on a release. active_inputs tracks the source devices: handle_event
drops a physical release whose capability is not in it ("Blocked up
event") before the intercept logic runs.

So an injected tap of a button that is physically held breaks the
intercept bookkeeping. HinterOS hit this with Guide in Pass mode: the
physical press switches to Always and puts Guide in
intercept_active_inputs; the session daemon sets Pass and taps Guide for
Steam through SendEvent; the tap's release takes Guide off active_inputs;
the physical release is then blocked, Guide stays in
intercept_active_inputs, and the next physical Guide press is dropped as
"already in the list". Its release clears that, so every other long press
was lost. Same in v0.83.0 and master (2026-10).
Upstream: https://github.com/ShadowBlip/InputPlumber
(src/input/composite_device/mod.rs, fn write_send_event).

--- a/src/input/composite_device/mod.rs
+++ b/src/input/composite_device/mod.rs
@@ -1134,8 +1134,10 @@
 
     /// Handles writing events that come from the dbus send_event interface
     async fn write_send_event(&mut self, event: NativeEvent) -> Result<(), Box<dyn Error>> {
-        let cap = event.as_capability();
-        self.is_new_active_event(&cap, event.pressed());
+        // Not is_new_active_event: active_inputs tracks the source devices, and an
+        // injected event must not change it. An injected release of a button that is
+        // still physically held would take it off the list, and the physical release
+        // would then be dropped ("Blocked up event") before the intercept logic sees it.
         // Check to see if the event is in recently translated.
         // If it is, spawn a task to delay emit the event.
         let sleep_time = Duration::from_millis(4);
